---
/** Plasterboard vs fibro: two cross-sections and the tells. */
import Cite from './Cite.astro';
---
<div class="idg">
  <article class="idg__card idg__card--ok">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" class="idg__svg" role="img" aria-label="Cross-section of plasterboard: a thick white gypsum core with a thin paper liner on each face">
      <rect x="20" y="60" width="280" height="60" fill="#f6f3ea" stroke="#8d97a8" />
      <rect x="20" y="54" width="280" height="6" fill="#d9cdb4" />
      <rect x="20" y="120" width="280" height="6" fill="#d9cdb4" />
      <g fill="#8d97a8" opacity="0.35"><circle cx="60" cy="80" r="2" /><circle cx="110" cy="100" r="2" /><circle cx="170" cy="75" r="2" /><circle cx="230" cy="105" r="2" /><circle cx="270" cy="85" r="2" /></g>
      <line x1="150" y1="40" x2="150" y2="57" stroke="#14316b" stroke-width="1.5" /><text x="150" y="34" text-anchor="middle" class="idg__t">paper face</text>
      <line x1="90" y1="150" x2="90" y2="90" stroke="#14316b" stroke-width="1.5" /><text x="90" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="idg__t">gypsum core · soft and chalky</text>
    </svg>
    <h3>Plasterboard — in the bin</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Paper on both faces, usually grey or ivory</li>
      <li>White or grey chalky core showing at cut edges</li>
      <li>Light for its size; a maker’s name is often printed on the back</li>
      <li>Standard, fire-rated and moisture-resistant types are all fine<Cite id="types" /></li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <article class="idg__card idg__card--no">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 320 180" class="idg__svg" role="img" aria-label="Cross-section of fibre cement sheet: a thin, dense grey sheet with no paper, dimpled on the back">
      <rect x="20" y="78" width="280" height="24" fill="#b9bfc7" stroke="#566074" />
      <g fill="#8d97a8"><circle cx="50" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="80" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="110" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="140" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="170" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="200" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="230" cy="106" r="2.5" /><circle cx="260" cy="106" r="2.5" /></g>
      <line x1="150" y1="40" x2="150" y2="76" stroke="#a3341f" stroke-width="1.5" /><text x="150" y="34" text-anchor="middle" class="idg__t idg__t--no">no paper · hard, cement-like</text>
      <line x1="90" y1="150" x2="90" y2="110" stroke="#a3341f" stroke-width="1.5" /><text x="90" y="166" text-anchor="middle" class="idg__t idg__t--no">often dimpled or textured on the back</text>
    </svg>
    <h3>Fibro — never in the bin</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Thin, hard, grey, cement-like sheet with no paper face</li>
      <li>Heavier than it looks; dimpled or textured back</li>
      <li>Common in eaves, wet areas, sheds and outside walls of older buildings</li>
      <li>Made before 1987, it contained around 15% asbestos<Cite id="fibro" /></li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</div>
<div class="note note--warn idg__note"><p><strong>Not sure? It stays out.</strong> Don’t cut, snap or sand a sheet to check — if it is fibro, that releases fibres. Only a lab test confirms asbestos; handle anything doubtful the way your site or council requires.</p></div>

<style>
  .idg { display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  @media (min-width: 820px) { .idg { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .idg__card { background: var(--paper); border-radius: var(--radius-m); padding: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem); border-top: 6px solid var(--green); }
  .idg__card--no { border-top-color: var(--danger); }
  .idg__svg { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .idg__t { font: 600 13px var(--font-body); fill: var(--navy); } .idg__t--no { fill: var(--danger); }
  .idg__card h3 { font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .idg__card ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; } .idg__card li { margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
  .idg__note { margin-top: 1.25rem; max-width: none; }
</style>
